Learn SQL From Scratch: 7 Essential SQL Queries Every Beginner Should Know
SQL (Structured Query Language) is an important skill for students, software developers, data analysts, and freshers preparing for technical interviews. If you are new to SQL, here are seven useful SQL queries explained with simple examples. For these examples, imagine we have a students table containing id, name, marks, department, and department_id columns. SELECT * FROM students;
This displays all records and columns from the students table. SELECT * FROM students WHERE marks > 80;
This displays students who scored more than 80 marks. SELECT * FROM students ORDER BY marks DESC;
This sorts students from highest to lowest marks. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM students;
This returns the total number of student records. …

Here's a pattern that's probably in your codebase:
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const result = await doTheWork(request);

  // Don't make the user wait for bookkeeping.
  void recordAudit({ userId, ok: true });
  void recordCost({ workspaceId, tokens: result.tokens });

  return Response.json(result);
}

It reads as "start these writes, don't wait for them". On a long-running Node server, that's what happens. On a serverless platform, it's a hope.
We noticed our AI cost ledger had no rows for days on end, even though AI calls were clearly happening. So we ran two identical requests against production from a fresh test account and watched the database:
The first request's audit row arrived about 18 seconds after its response, presumably when the function instance was wok…

UK graduate recruitment runs on dates. Spring weeks open in September, close in November, and a candidate who finds out in January is a year late. So we built four tables of them: spring weeks, summer internships, graduate schemes and law vacation schemes.
179 rows, 101 employers, 2,289 lines in one data file. The interesting part is not the scraping, because there was none. It is the type.
The naive model is opens: string | null. That null is doing at least three jobs, and the difference between them is the whole value of the page:
export type DeadlineCell =
  | { kind: 'date'; iso: string; reportedBy?: string }
  /** Opening only: accepting applications when checked, with no opening date published. */
  | { kind: 'open' }
  /** Closing only: reviewed as applications arrive, with no fixed…

PubTrivia sends product update emails to venues who opted in. Those emails need an unsubscribe link that works for a person who is not signed in, who may be reading in a mail client that will never show them a browser, and whose message has already been opened and refetched by two or three security scanners before they see it.
That is four constraints, and they decide the whole design. Here is what we ended up with, and the live endpoints you can poke at while you read.
The authorisation is an HMAC of the user id, and nothing else:
function sign(userId: string, key: string): string {
  return createHmac('sha256', key).update(`unsubscribe:${userId}`).digest('hex')
}

64 hex characters. The link carries the user id in the clear and the signature beside it:
https://pub-trivia.app/unsubscribe?…

