What happened
I got a request along these lines: "Our dev server is sitting idle — let's put an AI agent on it and automate some work." The target was a small e-commerce site, and the first candidate everyone thought of was automating order fulfillment (shipping updates). Before building anything, I decided to look at the numbers. I aggregated the last 180 days of orders: SELECT COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(payment_method = 'credit') / COUNT(*) AS credit_ratio FROM dtb_order WHERE order_date >= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL 180 DAY AND order_status NOT IN ('cancelled');
The result: 39 orders in 180 days — one or two a week. About 70% were card payments, and the payment itself is handled automatically by the payment processor. The only manual work is the shipping update, which adds up to r…

Tailwind v4's default colors and shadcn/ui's theme variables are written in oklch(). If your brand palette lives in hex, at some point you convert it. Online converters work, but I wanted to see the math and check the numbers against a palette I know well, so here's a small converter and what the output taught me.
The palette is from Northwind Roasters, a fictional coffee shop site I use for demos: cream background, terracotta primary, sage green accent, dark brown text.
OKLCH is OKLab in polar form, and OKLab comes from Björn Ottosson's published matrices. From hex: undo the sRGB gamma, go to an LMS-like space, take cube roots, go to Lab, then turn a/b into chroma and hue.
const lin = (c) => (c <= 0.04045 ? c / 12.92 : ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ** 2.4);

function hexToOklch(hex) {
  const [r,…

Buying an AC is a guessing game. You buy one too small, and it runs all day without cooling the room. You buy one too big, and it cycles on and off, leaving the room humid and wasting energy. Let's look at how to automate the BTU calculation using JavaScript. Calculate the Base BTUs: Square Footage × 20 = Base BTUs
Adjust for Sunlight: If the room gets full sun, add 10%. If heavily shaded, subtract 10%. Adjust for Occupants: Add 600 BTUs for every person beyond the first two. Adjust for Kitchens: If it's a kitchen, add 4,000 BTUs. Here is how you write a function to do this automatically: function calculateACBTUs(lengthFt, widthFt, sunLevel, occupants, isKitchen) { // 1. Base BTUs (20 BTUs per sq ft) const squareFeet = lengthFt * widthFt; let baseBTUs = squareFeet * 20;
// 2. Su…

Speech-language pathologists measure a child's MLU, mean length of utterance, by counting morphemes, not words. "Doggie jumped" is two words but three morphemes: doggie, jump, -ed. On a 50-utterance sample that counting is slow, so the MLU calculator I maintain has an "auto-mark" button: it rewrites jumped as jump/ed, and every slash adds one morpheme to the count. The clinician then fixes whatever it got wrong.
This post is about how little code that takes, and where it breaks.
Three suffix rules, three exception lists, and two guards for capitalised words:
